'''toft<sup>1</sup>''' (tôft), ''n.'' [Also ''tuft'' (see ''tuft''<sup>1</sup>); < ME. ''toft'' (AL. ''toftum''), < Icel. ''toft'', ''topt'', ''tupt'', ''tomt'', a knoll, a clearing, a cleared space, an inclosed piece of ground, = Norw. ''tomt'', ''tuft'' = Sw. ''tomt'', a clearing, ''toft'', the site of a house, = Dan. ''toft'', an inclosed field near a house; lit. an empty space, < Icel. ''tōmt'' (= Sw. ''tomt''), a neut. of ''tōmr'' = Sw. ''tom'', etc., empty: see ''toom''.] 1. A hillock; a slightly elevated and exposed site; open ground. [Obsolete or prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|As I beo-heold in-to the est an-heiȝ to the sonne,
